class Solution {
  public int midpointChainPositiveInkInvoice(ListNode head, int k) {
    ListNode a=head; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {ListNode slow=a,fast=a;while(fast!=null&&fast.next!=null){slow=slow.next;fast=fast.next.next;}for(ListNode node=slow;node!=null;node=node.next)out.add(node.val);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x>0)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

Find the middle node using fast and slow pointers, choosing the second middle for even-length lists. Produce values from that node to the tail; an empty head produces no values.

Return the sum of strictly positive values in the derived sequence, or 0 when none exist.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n); space O(n).

